  "recommendation": "approve",
  "summary": "Code-quality lane at PR #470 head 7c6e241: no blocking maintainability or false-success defects in the publish-on-validate, round grouping, fail-closed reuse, or stats paths. Per-result publish withholds only on validation/head/lock failures; incomplete rounds stay non-approvable on read. Residual advisories: stale fail-closed comment plus unused missing-sibling return, process.cwd()-based evidence path formatting, and GitHub/GitLab lane runId identity asymmetry around expectedLanes.",
  "blockers": [],
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "cq-stale-sibling-fail-closed-comment",
      "severity": "advisory",
      "location": {
        "path": "products/aie/src/app/pr_review_publish.ts",
        "line": 243,
        "endLine": 256,
        "side": "destination"
      },
      "message": "loadSiblingSynthesisLanes documents two opposing fail-closed rules: the function header correctly says missing/invalid siblings no longer withhold publication, but the catch branch still claims a non-reuse sibling \"fails publication closed\". The returned missing list is also discarded by the only call site, so the comment describes dead control flow and can mislead maintainers about the withhold boundary.",
      "suggestion": "Update the catch-branch comment to match the new per-result-only withhold rule, and either drop the unused missing return or surface missing siblings in diagnostics without reintroducing withhold.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    },
    {
      "id": "cq-cwd-evidence-path-format",
      "severity": "advisory",
      "location": {
        "path": "products/aie/src/app/pr_review_publish.ts",
        "line": 259,
        "endLine": 261,
        "side": "destination"
      },
      "message": "laneEvidenceFailure formats evidence paths with relativeEvidencePath(process.cwd(), path) instead of the publish repoRoot. When cwd differs from the configured repository root, failure messages can leak absolute paths or point at the wrong relative location, which is brittle path handling in a provider-facing failure path.",
      "suggestion": "Thread repoRoot into laneEvidenceFailure (and the snapshot-cache lock error that also uses process.cwd()) so relative paths are always repo-rooted.",
      "confidence": 0.78
    },
    {
      "id": "cq-provider-runid-expected-lanes-asymmetry",
      "severity": "advisory",
      "location": {
        "path": "adapters/gitlab/src/gitlab_review_forge.ts",
        "line": 209,
        "endLine": 214,
        "side": "destination"
      },
      "message": "GitHub stableLaneRunId hashes expectedLanes into the lane run id, while GitLab laneRunId hashes only head/lane/issue/pr. Skip-match still keys on round (which includes expectedLanes), so behavior is not false-success, but runId identity is provider-specific and makes cross-provider debugging and replay harder than necessary.",
      "suggestion": "Include the normalized expected lane set in GitLab laneRunId the same way GitHub does, or document and test the intentional divergence.",
      "confidence": 0.72
    }
  ],
